<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="How hydration works at a glance">
  <div><strong>1. Take it in</strong><span>Water comes from beverages, food and a smaller amount produced by metabolism.</span></div>
  <div><strong>2. Move it</strong><span>The gastrointestinal tract absorbs water and solutes into the circulation, where they are distributed throughout the body.</span></div>
  <div><strong>3. Regulate it</strong><span>Thirst and the kidneys continually adjust intake and urinary water loss in response to the body’s needs.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The hydration cycle</p>
  <h2>From a sip of water to whole-body balance</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Water enters through food and drink</h3><p>Most replacement water is consumed. The amount needed on a given day changes with food, activity, temperature, humidity, health and individual losses.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>The gut absorbs water and dissolved solutes</h3><p>Water can move across the intestinal lining, while transport of sodium, glucose and other absorbed solutes helps create osmotic gradients that influence net water movement.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>The circulation distributes what was absorbed</h3><p>Absorbed water enters extracellular fluid, including blood plasma and interstitial fluid, and exchanges with water inside cells according to osmotic forces.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Cells and extracellular fluid stay in osmotic communication</h3><p>Cell membranes are highly permeable to water. Changes in the concentration of dissolved particles outside cells can therefore shift water into or out of cells.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>The brain and kidneys defend water balance</h3><p>Changes in plasma osmolality and circulating volume influence thirst and arginine vasopressin. Vasopressin acts on the kidneys to increase water reabsorption when conservation is needed.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Losses restart the cycle</h3><p>Water leaves through urine, skin, breathing and the gastrointestinal tract. Exercise, heat, fever, vomiting or diarrhoea can increase losses and change replacement needs.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/3124674/pexels-photo-3124674.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1400" alt="Woman drinking water outdoors" width="1400" height="950" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Two related controls</p>
    <h2>Concentration and volume are not the same thing</h2>
    <p>The body is simultaneously managing the <strong>concentration</strong> of dissolved particles in body fluids and the <strong>volume</strong> of fluid in the circulation and extracellular space.</p>
    <p>Plasma osmolality is a powerful signal for thirst and vasopressin release. Changes in effective circulating volume also influence water- and sodium-conserving responses. These systems overlap, but they are not interchangeable.</p>
    <p>This distinction helps explain why hydration is not simply “more water is better.” Too little water can create problems, but excessive intake relative to solute balance and excretion can also be unsafe.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The kidney’s role</p>
    <h2>Your kidneys continuously fine-tune water output</h2>
    <p>The kidneys filter large amounts of fluid and then reclaim most of it. The final amount of water lost in urine is adjusted across the nephron. When vasopressin rises, aquaporin-2 water channels in the collecting duct become more available, increasing water reabsorption and allowing more concentrated urine.</p>
    <p>When the body has ample water and vasopressin falls, the kidneys can excrete more dilute urine. This flexibility is one reason healthy humans can tolerate a wide range of daily water intake.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Important</span>
    <h3>Urine colour is useful context, not a diagnosis.</h3>
    <p>Urine concentration can change with fluid intake, foods, supplements, medications and timing. In sport, trends in thirst, morning body mass, urine and session-specific sweat losses can be more informative together than any single marker alone.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Absorption</p>
    <h2>Water follows physiology, not marketing categories</h2>
    <p>Plain water can be entirely appropriate for ordinary hydration. In settings with greater fluid and electrolyte losses, a drink that also supplies relevant solutes may be useful. In medical oral rehydration, the coupled transport of sodium and glucose is deliberately used to support intestinal absorption.</p>
    <p>The right formulation depends on the purpose. A routine beverage, a sports drink and a therapeutic oral rehydration solution are not automatically interchangeable simply because all contain water and electrolytes.</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__button" href="/pages/glucose-water-absorption">Explore glucose &amp; water absorption</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">What changes your needs?</p>
  <h2>Hydration requirements are situational</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Heat &amp; sweat</h3><p>More sweating generally means greater water and sodium losses, but sweat rate and composition vary widely.</p><a href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Heat &amp; high sweat</a></article>
    <article><h3>Exercise duration</h3><p>Longer or more intense sessions can make fluid, sodium and carbohydrate planning more relevant.</p><a href="/pages/endurance-hydration">Endurance hydration</a></article>
    <article><h3>Illness</h3><p>Vomiting, diarrhoea, fever and poor intake can create clinically important fluid and electrolyte losses.</p><a href="/pages/clinical-hydration">Clinical hydration</a></article>
    <article><h3>Age &amp; health</h3><p>Thirst perception, kidney function, medications and health conditions can affect hydration strategy.</p><a href="/pages/active-aging">Active aging</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

